\label{sec:generalization}

We test whether the atlas results (69.6--85.4\%) reflect genuine regularities or training artifacts along seven axes: classifier choice (\S\ref{sec:gen:baselines}), cross-model (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossmodel}), cross-strategy (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossstrategy}), and cross-scale transfer (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossscale}) below; three additional axes in the Appendix.

\subsection{Classifier Comparison and Surface-Text Ablation}
\label{sec:gen:baselines}

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} evaluates RF (bagging), XGBoost, and LightGBM (gradient boosting) on the same 19 features; per-cell spread $\leq$0.4pp establishes a feature-determined ceiling.
On Pythia nucleus, CORAL \cite{coral2020} (78.1\%) and DivEye \cite{diveye2026} (78.2\%) match within-cell RF within 0.1\,pp ($p>0.05$); all feature-based methods outperform CE-MLP (74.9\%, $p<0.001$) and logistic regression (75.0\%).

\paragraph{Surface-text ablation.}
Single-feature regression on mean perplexity performs at chance (25.1\%); two zero-shot LLMs (DeepSeek V3.2: 36.2\%, Qwen2.5-7B: 30.4\%; Appendix~\ref{app:zeroshot_prompt}) also fail, confirming depth signal resides in distributional statistics inaccessible from surface text (42\,pp gap vs.\ RF).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Classifier convergence: 4-class accuracy (\%) across three atlas cells on the same 19 features. $\Delta_{\max}$: largest per-cell spread. Chance = 25\%.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
Cell & RF & XGB & LGBM & $\Delta_{\max}$ \\
\midrule
Pythia, nuc. & 78.8 & 79.0 & 78.9 & 0.2 \\
GPT-2~XL, $\tau$ & 85.8 & 86.2 & 86.2 & 0.4 \\
Pythia, top-$k$ & 68.9 & 68.9 & 68.7 & 0.2 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Convergence across five classifiers confirms the signal resides in features, not the algorithm (Figure~\ref{fig:confusion_matrix}); errors concentrate at d2/d3 in-distribution and spread to d1--d3 under transfer.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_confusion_matrix.pdf}
\caption{Confusion matrices: best in-distribution (GPT-2 XL, $\tau{=}0.9$, 85.4\%) and best cross-model transfer (OLMo holdout, 69.2\%). d0 recall $>$99\% in both; errors spread to d1--d3 under transfer.}
\label{fig:confusion_matrix}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Cross-Model Transfer}
\label{sec:gen:crossmodel}

We perform leave-one-model-family-out evaluation (Table~\ref{tab:cross_model}): train on two families (nucleus), test on the held-out third.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-model transfer (leave-one-out, nucleus). Chance = 25\%/33\% (d1--d3). Mean d1--d3 bal.\ acc: 55.4\%.}
\label{tab:cross_model}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Holdout & Acc & d0 & d1 & d2 & d3 \\
 & (\%) & Rec. & Rec. & Rec. & Rec. \\
\midrule
Pythia & 62.4 & 100.0 & 49.3 & 26.8 & 73.6 \\
OLMo & \textbf{69.2} & 99.3 & 75.5 & 43.3 & 58.8 \\
GPT-2 XL & 67.6 & 98.5 & 74.7 & 52.6 & 44.5 \\
\midrule
Mean & 66.4 & 99.3 & 66.5 & 40.9 & 59.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Mean transfer accuracy is 66.4\% (2.7$\times$ chance); d0 recall ($>$98\%) inflates the headline while d1--d3 balanced accuracy averages 55.4\% (chance = 33\%), with d2 dropping to 26.8\% (Pythia holdout).

\subsection{Cross-Strategy Transfer}
\label{sec:gen:crossstrategy}

Leave-one-strategy-out results appear in Table~\ref{tab:cross_strategy}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-strategy transfer (leave-one-out). Chance = 25\% / 33\% (d1--d3). d0 recall $\geq$89\% inflates headline; d1--d3 mean: 45.9\%.}
\label{tab:cross_strategy}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Holdout & Pythia & OLMo & GPT-2 XL \\
 & Acc (\%) & Acc (\%) & Acc (\%) \\
\midrule
nucleus & \textbf{69.4} & \textbf{65.4} & \textbf{59.9} \\
temp ($\tau{=}0.9$) & 62.8 & 58.2 & 56.1 \\
top-$k$ ($k{=}50$) & 51.5 & 54.9 & 53.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Mean cross-strategy accuracy (59.1\%) falls 7pp below cross-model (66.4\%); excluding d0, d1--d3 accuracy is 45.9\% (chance = 33\%).
d2 recall varies sharply (nucleus holdouts 30--54\%, top-$k$ 8--19\%, temperature 3.7--39.5\%).
Training on nucleus + top-$k$ and testing on temperature inverts the d1/d2 boundary (d1v2 AUC $<$ 0.5); per-strategy z-score normalization partially recovers it (Limitations).
A multi-temperature ablation (Appendix~\ref{app:multi_tau}) confirms within-$\tau$ signal holds for $\tau \leq 0.9$ but collapses at $\tau{=}1.1$; cross-$\tau$ LOO transfer (mean 46.7\%) is harder than cross-strategy transfer.

End-to-end PLMs (DeBERTa-v3, RoBERTa) achieve only ${\sim}$59\% (19pp below RF), confirming depth signal is inaccessible to standard PLM representations (Appendix~\ref{app:e2e}).

