Both exceed chance: Llama-3.1-8B achieves 70.2\% (95\% CI $[68.3, 72.4]$, $\mathrm{EDD}_{0.70}{=}4$) and Qwen2.5-7B 60.1\% ($[58.0, 62.1]$, $\mathrm{EDD}_{0.70}{>}5$), with weaker per-step signal (mean consecutive AUC ${\approx}$0.78 vs.\ 0.85 at 1--1.5B).

\paragraph{Base vs.\ Instruction-Tuned Control.}
A base-model control (Llama-3.1-8B) achieves 59.9\% (95\% CI [57.8, 62.15]) with non-monotonic AUC decay ($d_{2{\text{v}}3}=0.682 \to d_{3{\text{v}}4}=0.730$), while the instruction-tuned variant reaches 70.2\% with strictly monotonic decay---suggesting instruction-tuning \emph{regularizes} degradation patterns rather than introducing confounding artifacts.

Four confounds (quantization, reduced $n$, single strategy, vocabulary mismatch) limit causal attribution (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_scale_full}).
Cross-model recursive chains (84.75\%; Appendix~\ref{app:cross_boundary}) and cross-domain transfer (arXiv 74.1\%) further confirm signal robustness.

